# Deploy and drive

With hardware verified and offsets captured, wire YAGSL into your robot code. `SwerveParser` reads your `swerve/base` config directory and builds a YAMS `SwerveDrive` for you. You only write the subsystem (Commands v2) or mechanism (Commands v3) and the control bindings around it.

{% hint style="info" %}
The entry point depends on the vendordep you installed in [step 2](/tutorial/02-install-yagsl.md): `swervelib.commands2.SwerveParser` returns a `yams.commands2.swerve.SwerveDrive`, and `swervelib.commands3.SwerveParser` returns a `yams.commands3.swerve.SwerveDrive`. Everything else about the two is the same. WPILib 2027 classes live under `org.wpilib.*`.
{% endhint %}

## Create a swerve subsystem

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
package first.robot.subsystems.swervedrive;

import java.io.File;
import java.util.function.DoubleSupplier;
import org.wpilib.command2.Command;
import org.wpilib.command2.Commands;
import org.wpilib.command2.SubsystemBase;
import org.wpilib.math.geometry.Pose2d;
import org.wpilib.math.geometry.Rotation2d;
import org.wpilib.system.Filesystem;
import swervelib.commands2.SwerveParser;
import yams.commands2.config.SwerveDriveConfig;
import yams.commands2.swerve.SwerveDrive;
import yams.commands2.swerve.SwerveInputStream;
import yams.core.telemetry.SwerveDriveTelemetryConfig;
import yams.core.telemetry.enums.TelemetryVerbosity;

public class SwerveDriveSubsystem extends SubsystemBase
{

  private final SwerveDrive drive;

  public SwerveDriveSubsystem()
  {
    var cfg = new SwerveDriveConfig()
        .withStartingPose(new Pose2d(3, 3, Rotation2d.ZERO))
        .withSubsystem(this)
        .withTelemetry("swerve", new SwerveDriveTelemetryConfig(TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH));

    SwerveParser.parse(new File(Filesystem.getDeployDirectory(), "swerve/base"));
    drive = SwerveParser.createSwerveDrive(cfg);
  }

  public SwerveInputStream getAngularVelocityStream(DoubleSupplier x, DoubleSupplier y,
                                                    DoubleSupplier rot)
  {
    return SwerveInputStream.of(drive, x, y, rot);
  }

  /** Drive with a field relative SwerveInputStream. */
  public Command driveFieldRelative(SwerveInputStream stream)
  {
    return run(() -> drive.setFieldRelativeChassisSpeeds(stream.get())).withName("Drive Field Relative");
  }

  /** Zero the gyro heading. Bind this to a button combo for field recovery. */
  public Command zeroGyro()
  {
    return Commands.runOnce(drive::zeroGyro, this).withName("Zero Gyro");
  }

  @Override
  public void periodic()
  {
    drive.updateTelemetry();
  }

  @Override
  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    drive.simIterate();
  }
}
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}

```java
package first.robot.mechanisms;

import java.io.File;
import org.wpilib.command3.Command;
import org.wpilib.command3.Mechanism;
import org.wpilib.math.geometry.Pose2d;
import org.wpilib.math.geometry.Rotation2d;
import org.wpilib.math.kinematics.ChassisVelocities;
import org.wpilib.system.Filesystem;
import swervelib.commands3.SwerveParser;
import yams.commands3.config.SwerveDriveConfig;
import yams.commands3.swerve.SwerveDrive;
import yams.core.telemetry.SwerveDriveTelemetryConfig;
import yams.core.telemetry.enums.TelemetryVerbosity;

public class SwerveDriveMechanism implements Mechanism
{

  private final SwerveDrive drive;

  public SwerveDriveMechanism()
  {
    var cfg = new SwerveDriveConfig()
        .withStartingPose(new Pose2d(3, 3, Rotation2d.ZERO))
        .withMechanism(this)
        .withTelemetry("swerve", new SwerveDriveTelemetryConfig(TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH));

    SwerveParser.parse(new File(Filesystem.getDeployDirectory(), "swerve/base"));
    drive = SwerveParser.createSwerveDrive(cfg);
  }

  /** The underlying YAMS SwerveDrive, used to build a SwerveInputStream. */
  public SwerveDrive getDrive()
  {
    return drive;
  }

  /** Drive with field relative ChassisVelocities. */
  public void drive(ChassisVelocities velocity)
  {
    drive.setFieldRelativeChassisSpeeds(velocity);
  }

  /** Zero the gyro heading. Bind this to a button combo for field recovery. */
  public Command zeroGyro()
  {
    return run(coroutine -> drive.zeroGyro()).named("Zero Gyro");
  }

  /** Call every loop. Commands v3 mechanisms have no periodic() hook of their own. */
  public void periodic()
  {
    drive.updateTelemetry();
  }

  /** Call every loop in simulation. */
  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    drive.simIterate();
  }
}
```

A Commands v3 `Mechanism` has no `periodic()` of its own, so call these from your robot class, before the scheduler runs:

```java
package first.robot;

import first.robot.mechanisms.SwerveDriveMechanism;
import org.wpilib.command3.Scheduler;
import org.wpilib.framework.OpModeRobot;

public class Robot extends OpModeRobot
{

  public final SwerveDriveMechanism swerve = new SwerveDriveMechanism();

  private final Scheduler scheduler = Scheduler.getDefault();

  @Override
  public void robotPeriodic()
  {
    swerve.periodic();
    scheduler.run();
  }

  @Override
  public void simulationPeriodic()
  {
    swerve.simulationPeriodic();
  }
}
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

`SwerveParser.parse(...)` reads `swervedrive.json` and every file it references under `modules/`. `SwerveParser.createSwerveDrive(cfg)` then resolves your hardware through the matching vendor library and hands back a fully constructed YAMS `SwerveDrive`. There's no constants file to maintain by hand. Both methods are static, so you never create a `SwerveParser` instance.

The name passed to `withTelemetry("swerve", ...)` is where the drive publishes in NetworkTables (`Mechanisms/swerve`), which is where step 4's dashboard values come from. `TelemetryVerbosity.HIGH` is useful while bringing your robot up but adds NetworkTables overhead, so turn it down once you've finished tuning.

{% hint style="info" %}
`SwerveDrive.getGyroAngle()` no longer exists. Read the robot's attitude with `drive.getGyroRotation3d()`, and use `drive.getGyroRotation3d().toRotation2d()` when you only need the heading. Driving with `setFieldRelativeChassisSpeeds(...)` as shown above needs neither: YAMS converts the field relative velocities with the gyro heading itself.
{% endhint %}

## Bind your controller

{% tabs %}
{% tab title="Commands v2" %}

```java
package first.robot;

import first.robot.subsystems.swervedrive.SwerveDriveSubsystem;
import org.wpilib.command2.button.CommandXboxController;
import yams.commands2.swerve.SwerveInputStream;

public class RobotContainer
{

  final CommandXboxController driverXbox = new CommandXboxController(0);

  private final SwerveDriveSubsystem swerve = new SwerveDriveSubsystem();

  // Xbox sticks read negative when pushed forward/left, so every axis is negated:
  // left stick forward drives away from the driver station, right stick right spins clockwise.
  private final SwerveInputStream driveAngularVelocity =
      swerve.getAngularVelocityStream(
                () -> -driverXbox.getLeftY(),
                () -> -driverXbox.getLeftX(),
                () -> -driverXbox.getRightX())
            .withDeadband(0.05)
            .withAllianceRelativeControl();

  public RobotContainer()
  {
    configureBindings();
  }

  private void configureBindings()
  {
    // Default drive command
    swerve.setDefaultCommand(swerve.driveFieldRelative(driveAngularVelocity));

    // Zero the gyro with Menu + View. Use this if the field-relative heading drifts.
    driverXbox.menu().and(driverXbox.view()).onTrue(swerve.zeroGyro());
  }
}
```

{% endtab %}

{% tab title="Commands v3" %}
In Commands v3 the bindings live in a teleop opmode, so they only exist while that opmode is selected on the Driver Station. The drive command is a coroutine that loops until it is canceled:

```java
package first.robot.opmodes.teleop;

import first.robot.Robot;
import org.wpilib.command3.Command;
import org.wpilib.command3.button.CommandXboxController;
import org.wpilib.command3.button.RobotModeTriggers;
import org.wpilib.driverstation.XboxController;
import org.wpilib.opmode.OpMode;
import org.wpilib.opmode.Teleop;
import yams.commands3.swerve.SwerveInputStream;

@Teleop(name = "Angular Velocity Control")
public class AngularVelocityTeleop implements OpMode
{

  private final CommandXboxController driverXbox = new CommandXboxController(0);
  private final SwerveInputStream     driveStream;

  public AngularVelocityTeleop(Robot robot)
  {
    // Xbox sticks read negative when pushed forward/left, so every axis is negated.
    XboxController hid = driverXbox.getController();
    driveStream = SwerveInputStream.of(robot.swerve.getDrive(), () -> -hid.getLeftY(), () -> -hid.getLeftX())
        .withControllerRotationAxis(() -> -hid.getRightX())
        .withDeadband(0.05)
        .withAllianceRelativeControl();

    Command driveCommand = robot.swerve.run(coroutine -> {
      while (true)
      {
        robot.swerve.drive(driveStream.get());
        coroutine.yield();
      }
    }).named("Drive Angular Velocity");

    RobotModeTriggers.teleop().whileTrue(driveCommand);

    // Zero the gyro with Menu + View. Use this if the field-relative heading drifts.
    driverXbox.menu().and(driverXbox.view()).onTrue(robot.swerve.zeroGyro());
  }
}
```

{% endtab %}
{% endtabs %}

{% hint style="info" %}
**Why bind `zeroGyro()` to a button combo?** Gyros can drift, or power on facing the wrong direction. A button combo (Menu + View, or both bumpers) lets the driver instantly re-align field-relative control without touching the Driver Station. Always bind this. It has saved matches.
{% endhint %}

Complete, buildable projects for both command frameworks are in the [YAGSL examples](https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAGSL/tree/main/examples), and are attached to every [release](https://github.com/Yet-Another-Software-Suite/YAGSL/releases) as `example-commands2.zip` and `example-commands3.zip`.

## First drive

1. Deploy your code to the SystemCore.
2. On blocks, enable the robot in Teleop and push the drive stick gently forward. All four wheels should point the same direction and the robot should attempt to move that way.
3. Rotate with the rotation axis. The robot should spin around its center, not drift while spinning.
4. If a module fights the others (spins to the wrong angle, or drives backward relative to the rest), revisit [step 4](/tutorial/04-verify-and-calibrate-hardware.md) for that module's inversion/offset.
5. Once it drives cleanly on blocks, test on the ground at low speed before opening it up.

Congratulations, you have a driving swerve robot. From here, see the **How-to Guides** section for [tuning PIDF gains](/how-to-guides/tune-pidf-gains.md), diagnosing drift, and other tasks you'll return to throughout the season.


